<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Korçë</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korça</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Korçë is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, on a plateau near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>. A centre of the Albanian national awakening, it saw the opening of the first Albanian-language school in 1887. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> French troops occupied it and in 1916 created an autonomous Albanian province there, which they handed to the Albanian government in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korce.jpg" alt="Korçë" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ShkelzenRexha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kor%C3%A7a_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Korçë stands on a plateau some 850 m above sea level, surrounded by the Morava Mountains. The current name is of Slavic origin: gorica means hill in the South Slavic languages and is a very common toponym. The city is known by other names, among them Goritsa in Bulgarian, Macedonian and Serbian, Korytsa in Greek, Coriza in Italian and Görice in Turkish. The Old Bazaar, with the Mirahori Mosque, is regarded as the urban core, and the town's actual physiognomy was realised in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Korçë's foundation is closely tied to Ilias Bey Mirahori, an Albanian convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Master of the Stables to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Sultan Bayezid II</a>. In 1484 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> rewarded him with seven villages in the Korçë area, and a series of firmans replaced these in 1497 with the locality of Piskopiye, whose boundaries correspond to the territory of today's city. When the nearby town of Moscopole was destroyed by Ali Pasha's men in 1769, some of its commerce moved to Korçë, and part of its population came from there. Greek schools were founded from 1724, and on the eve of the Balkan Wars 2,115 students attended Greek education in the city.</p><p>Ottoman rule lasted until 1912, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> declared its independence. Armed Albanian bands under Sali Butka, Spiro Bellkameni and Kajo Babjeni briefly liberated Korçë in August 1912. Because of its proximity to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the city was fiercely contested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, and Greek forces captured it on 6 December 1912. After 1916 the city came under French control, which lasted until 1920. Albanian bands led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistokli-germenji">Themistokli Gërmenji</a> and Sali Butka fought for Albanian self-administration against the Venizelist forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Korçë is the seat of Korçë County in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, with 51,152 inhabitants in the city at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It has been regarded as the intellectual capital of southern Albania. Fan Noli University was founded in 1971, and the National Museum of Medieval Art holds about 7,000 icons. Aromanian and Greek communities remain, and the largest carnival in Albania takes place there before Orthodox Easter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Korçë” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the First World War French forces of the Salonika front occupied <b>Korçë</b> in late 1916.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-albanian-republic-of-korce">Autonomous Albanian Republic of Korçë</a></li></ul></section>
